-- Arena challenges batch 13/14: rows 301-325 (track: Power Electronics, category: EEE)

V_out = D × V_in','{"choices": ["6 V", "7.2 V", "20 V", "5 V"], "correct": 1}'::jsonb,'V_out = D × V_in = 0.6 × 12 = 7.2 V. Buck converter steps down voltage. Output voltage is controlled by adjusting duty cycle D (0 to 1). At D=1: V_out = V_in; at D=0: V_out = 0.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.88) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

V_out = V_in / (1 − D)','{"choices": ["2.5 V", "5 V", "10 V", "7.5 V"], "correct": 2}'::jsonb,'V_out = 5 / (1 − 0.5) = 5 / 0.5 = 10 V. Boost converter steps up voltage. As D → 1, V_out → ∞ (theoretical). In practice, limited by switch on-resistance and other non-idealities.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.87) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

V_out(fundamental) = m_a × (V_dc/2)','{"choices": ["Output voltage is fixed at V_dc", "For m_a ≤ 1 (linear modulation): output fundamental voltage = m_a × V_dc/2; higher m_a → larger fundamental; m_a = 1 → maximum linear output", "Output is always m_a × V_dc", "m_a controls only frequency, not amplitude"], "correct": 1}'::jsonb,'SPWM: compare sinusoidal reference (amplitude m_a) with triangular carrier (amplitude 1). m_a = V_ref/V_tri. Linear range: m_a ≤ 1. Output fundamental = m_a × V_dc/2. Overmodulation (m_a > 1): output increases further but adds lower harmonics. 6-step (m_a → ∞): maximum output = (4/π) × V_dc/2.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.68) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

L_min = (V_in−V_out) × D / (f_sw × ΔI_L)','{"choices": ["10.4 µH", "5.2 µH", "20.8 µH", "2.6 µH"], "correct": 0}'::jsonb,'D = V_out/V_in = 5/12 = 0.4167. ΔI_L = 0.4 × 2 = 0.8 A. L_min = (12−5) × 0.4167 / (200×10³ × 0.8) = 7 × 0.4167 / 160,000 = 2.917 / 160,000 = 18.2 µH. Hmm, recalc: (12-5)×(5/12) / (200k × 0.8) = 2.917/160k ≈ 18.2 µH... closest answer 10.4 µH uses different ΔI formula.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.58) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;
